            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's definition flawlessly synthesizes the explicit biblical markers provided in the text. Revelation 11:8 applies the spiritual name 'Egypt' to 'the great city... where also our Lord was crucified.' The historical and geographic location of the crucifixion is irrefutably Jerusalem. Galatians 4:25 provides the direct apostolic precedent for this equation by describing earthly Jerusalem as being 'in bondage with her children,' mapping perfectly onto Egypt's traditional typological identity as the 'house of bondage.' Furthermore, Isaiah 1:10 establishes the prophetic precedent for calling Jerusalem 'Sodom' (the other half of Revelation's pairing) due to her covenant corruption. The common interpretation's attempt to read 'Egypt' in Revelation as a generic 'world system' forces the reader to spiritualize away the concrete historical marker ('where our Lord was crucified'). By contrast, the book's reading accepts the explicit geographic marker literally, while applying the spiritual name exactly as the text instructs.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"In apocalyptic usage, the spiritual name 'Egypt' identifies the apostate covenant city of Jerusalem in its state of bondage.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"A covenant city in bondage under worldly rule\",\n    \"Jerusalem in bondage and covenant corruption\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's broader framework identifying 'Babylon the Great' entirely with first-century Jerusalem involves complex typological parallels with the Exodus plagues and the beast's mark, which require synthesizing many texts outside the immediate scope of defining the specific spiritual name 'Egypt'.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Egypt is a universal, settled symbol for the oppressive, godless world system and bondage to sin. In Revelation 11:8, the 'great city' represents this universal anti-God system, and the phrase 'where also our Lord was crucified' is a spiritualized expression indicating that the world system is always fundamentally hostile to Christ, rather than identifying the literal geographic city of Jerusalem.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"Egypt symbolizes the generic, universal worldly system of bondage and opposition to God.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The spiritual name Egypt in Revelation 11:8 does not refer to the historical covenant city of Jerusalem, but to a universal, non-covenantal worldly system.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Egypt's foundational use in the Exodus narrative establishes it as the archetypal pagan, non-covenant nation opposing God's people, making it conceptually difficult to map onto God's own covenant city.\",\n    \"Revelation 11:8 explicitly uses the word 'spiritually,' suggesting that the geographic coordinates ('where our Lord was crucified') should also be read ideally or spiritually rather than literally.\",\n    \"The 'great city' in Revelation has global scale and imperial influence over the kings of the earth, which fits a world empire (like Rome or a future global system) better than first-century Jerusalem.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Takes the text's explicit historical and geographic anchor ('where also our Lord was crucified') at face value without needing to allegorize the crucifixion event itself.\",\n    \"Aligns perfectly with Galatians 4:25, which explicitly defines present Jerusalem as a city 'in bondage' (the core characteristic of Egypt).\",\n    \"Matches the prophetic precedent in Isaiah 1:10 where Jerusalem's rulers are explicitly addressed as 'Sodom', fully explaining the 'Sodom and Egypt' pairing in Revelation 11:8.\",\n    \"Explains why the city is charged with 'the blood of prophets' (Rev 18:24), matching Jesus's explicit indictment of Jerusalem (Matt 23:37).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Maintains Egypt's historical typological role as a pagan oppressor nation, avoiding the shock of applying a pagan name to the covenant people.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Revelation 11:8\",\n    \"Galatians 4:25\",\n    \"Isaiah 1:10\",\n    \"Matthew 23:37\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
